,title,link,text,language,family
0,Jemez language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jemez_language,"Jemez or Hemish (also Towa) is a Kiowa-Tanoan language spoken by the Jemez Pueblo people in New Mexico. It has no common written form, as tribal rules do not allow the language to be transcribed; linguists describing the language have used the Americanist phonetic notation with slight modifications.",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
1,Tanoan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanoan_languages,"Tanoan (/ təˈnoʊ.ən / tə-NOH-ən), also Kiowa-Tanoan or Tanoan-Kiowa, is a family of languages spoken by indigenous peoples in present-day New Mexico, Kansas, Oklahoma, and Texas.",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
2,Jemez language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/jemez_language,"The Jemez language, also known as Towa, is a Tanoan language belonging to the Kiowa-Tanoan language family, spoken primarily by the Jemez Pueblo people in north-central New Mexico, United States. [1] It serves as the primary language of the Jemez community, with approximately 3,000 speakers (as of 2024), representing about 91% of the population of around 3,400, including most children, making ...",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
3,"P' ǽ kish: The Jemez People - Pueblo - Jemez Pueblo, New Mexico, USA ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=haV9uDIS7fk,"The Pueblo of Jemez is a federally recognized tribe located in north-central New Mexico, approximately, 50 miles northwest of Albuquerque.",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
4,Towa Language and the Jemez Indian Pueblo,https://www.native-languages.org/towa.htm,"Towa (Jemez) Language Towa (also known as Jemez, after the pueblo where it is spoken) is one of three Kiowa-Tanoan languages spoken by the Pueblo people of New Mexico. Though these languages are closely related, speakers of one cannot fully understand speakers of another (similar to German and English speakers).",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
5,Black Eagle Singers - New Mexico Music Commission,https://newmexicomusic.org/2015/11/16/black-eagle-jemez-pueblo/,"The Black Eagle group has recorded a half-dozen different productions for various independent labels specializing in Native American or ""na"" music. Native American Jemez Pueblo, New Mexico is a remarkable production on Indian Sounds, followed up by a second volume that was the ensemble's first release on compact disc.",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
6,Black Eagle Singers legacy runs deep in Jemez Pueblo,https://www.koat.com/article/black-eagle-singers-legacy-runs-deep-in-jemez-pueblo-gathering-of-nations/64568660,"Black Eagle Singers legacy runs deep in Jemez Pueblo First started in 1989, the performance group has gone on to win a Grammy and create music highlighting the importance of music and their culture",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
7,Phonetics of Jemez vowels | The Journal of the Acoustical Society of ...,https://pubs.aip.org/asa/jasa/article/93/4_Supplement/2297/738676/Phonetics-of-Jemez-vowels,"This paper presents a spectral and durational analysis of the vowels of Jemez, a Kiowa-Tanoan language spoken northwest of Albuquerque. The vowel system consists of six short vowels /i e æ ɑ o (barred eye)/ and six corresponding long vowels. For each long and short vowel, except /e/ and /ee/, there is a corresponding nasal vowel.",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
8,Phonetics of Jemez vowels - Semantic Scholar,https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Phonetics-of-Jemez-vowels-Bell-Heins/7e39a20d15b2a58cd671c5909744a1a6f55de8c8,"This paper presents a spectral and durational analysis of the vowels of Jemez, a Kiowa-Tanoan language spoken northwest of Albuquerque. The vowel system consists of six short vowels /i e ae ɑ o (barred eye)/ and six corresponding long vowels. For each long and short vowel, except /e/ and /ee/, there is a corresponding nasal vowel. The principal data for the analysis consists of repetitions ...",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
9,Languages | Native American Pottery,https://www.eyesofthepot.com/languages.php,"In the Tanoan-Kiowa group, Towa is closer to Kiowa than to Tewa-Tiwa but all tongues in the group share a common ancestor. A proposal has been put forth that would convert the Tanoan-Kiowa family to a Tanoan-Aztecan family with a connection to the Uto-Aztecan family and the greater overview that would create would potentially bring the Zuni ...",Jemez,Kiowa-Tanoan
